
# Executive summary — headline changes & their intent

* **Memory evolution & reliability:** the module moved from LPDDR2 → DDR3L and later DDR3L variants were swapped to support larger density and (in rev-4) **ECC support**. Reason: capacity, availability and reliability/field robustness. ([Trenz Electronic][1])
* **Power supply / DC-DC changes:** Trenz replaced obsolete DC-DC regulators (EN5311QI → MPM3834CGPA) to solve EOL and increase current capability; they also adjusted power-up sequencing for improved reliability. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
* **Boot/config & tooling fixes:** added a **JTAG-only boot DIP switch** so customers can force JTAG boot when QSPI programming breaks with newer Vivado/tooling; renamed SPI boot signals to match AMD boot-mode conventions. These are practical fixes for toolchain/boot problems. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
* **BOM / availability / manufacturability changes:** multiple parts were swapped for EOL or cost/availability reasons (ferrite beads, common-mode filters, LEDs, capacitors/resistors). Testpoint sizes and fiducials standardized for manufacturing/test. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
* **Field/production housekeeping:** SPI flash device was replaced in 2025 (different part number and temperature grade); FTDI EEPROM content was updated in 2025 for Vivado / FTDI programming compatibility (identification / license key). These affect programming/production flows. ([Mouser Electronics][3])

