# Chronological, source-backed breakdown (PCN → what changed → why → impact)

### PCN-20210121 — *“TE0726-03 DDR3 Change and Product Update”* (issue date **2021-01-25**)

* **What changed:** DDR3 RAM part number was changed (old → new), and a few clock device revisions and LED part swaps for availability/cost. ([Trenz Electronic][4])
* **Why:** original DDR part reached EOL / availability issues; clock device revision improved long-term supply. ([Trenz Electronic][4])
* **Impact for you:** *possible* small timing differences — Trenz flags “new DDR timings may be considered” (i.e., if you rely on extremely timing/latency-tight DDR behavior you should validate). Otherwise no board-level change needed if you use the module as a black box. ([Trenz Electronic][4])

---

### PCN-20230619 — *“TE0726-03 → TE0726-04 Hardware Revision Change”* (issue date **2023-10-04**) — **major revision** (this is the main rev3→rev4 PCN)

This PCN is big — it lists ~20+ individual changes. I’ve grouped the important ones below:

* **DC-DC regulator swap (U16, U17, U19, U20):** EN5311QI → MPM3834CGPA (reason: EOL). Result: increased current capability and minor electrical differences. **Action:** respect new power characteristics; don’t alter sequencing without re-validation. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
* **DDR → ECC enablement:** DDR3 part changed to an ECC-capable device (IS43TR16256ECL...) and Trenz added I²C/ECC wiring, level shifters and buffers (R157/R158 options, U12, U24/U25). **Reason:** provide optional ECC for reliability. **Impact:** ECC is enabled in the module design path — firmware/boot images that assume the previous memory topology might need adaptation; ECC status lines are routed through local buffers/level shifters. If you rely on debugging DDR from the carrier, note the added ECC I²C dependency. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
* **Power-up sequencing change:** updated power sequence to match the new DC-DCs and improve reliability. **Action:** carrier must not break expected sequencing — preserve rail bring-up constraints. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
* **JTAG-only boot DIP (S1 / R152):** added to allow forcing JTAG boot because of QSPI programming problems with newer Vivado releases. This directly addresses Vivado/Quartus toolchain quirks (AR#00002 style). **Action:** include a JTAG/boot override on your carrier or replicate the same capability. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
* **PUDC dual-use change and DONE level-translate:** resistor changes and diode added to allow PUDC to be dual-purpose and to fix DONE voltage level translation between CPLD and FPGA — small but important for robust boot behavior. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
* **HDMI CEC removed / components DNP:** CEC circuit partly removed to improve HDMI behaviour; if you used CEC in firmware you’ll need to adjust. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
* **MIC bias & audio-related small add:** added mic bias circuit to allow alternative microphones. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
* **BOM optimizations / EOL substitutions:** changed common-mode filters (SP5001→EMI8042MUTAG), ferrite beads, capacitor and resistor value swaps, testpoint size standardization and new TP15–TP20 testpoints and standard fiducials for manufacturing. These are supply/manufacturing-driven changes and improve testability. ([Mouser Electronics][2])

(Production note in the same PCN: rev-4 production started after old stock was used; see PCN for dates.) ([Mouser Electronics][2])

