# Key design decisions Trenz made (why they took them — short list)

1. **Move away from smaller/older RAM types → DDR3L & ECC support** — availability, capacity and reliability for embedded Linux + PL workloads. ECC increases data integrity for long-running embedded systems. ([Trenz Electronic][4])
2. **Swap DC-DCs and optimize BOM** — hardware vendors must react to component EOL; Trenz chose modern, slightly higher-cap regulators (improves current margin). They also standardized testpoints/fiducials to make assembly/testing easier. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
3. **Tooling/boot practical fixes (JTAG switch, signal renaming)** — response to real user pain (QSPI programming with newer Vivado); making boot signals follow AMD naming reduces confusion and improves tool compatibility. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
4. **Keep module as SoM & evolve module internals** — Trenz preferred to keep the module footprint and update internals rather than break the module-to-carrier interface; that preserves backwards compatibility of many carrier boards. ([Trenz Electronic][1])

