# What you — as the carrier board (Zynq project) designer — must *absolutely* know & do

(Short checklist with why each item matters; each point includes the source.)

1. **Preserve expected power sequencing & rail behavior** — rev-4 uses new DC-DCs and an updated sequence. Don’t add circuits on the carrier that change the module rails’ rise times or interfere with POR/PUDC actions. *Why:* wrong sequencing bricks or prevents boot. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
2. **Provide a JTAG-only boot path / DIP or jumper** — Trenz added S1 to fix Vivado QSPI programming problems; include equivalent on your carrier so you can recover with JTAG if QSPI access fails. *Why:* makes initial bring-up and reprogramming robust. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
3. **If you depend on the module’s RAM behavior (timings / ECC), validate with the specific module batch** — PCNs changed DDR devices and enabled ECC; firmware may need updates. *Why:* DDR timing or ECC state can affect boot and memory tests. ([Trenz Electronic][4])
4. **Boot-flash awareness:** the 2025 SPI flash swap changed device IDs and temp grade — confirm programming tools (flash-detect logic) and thermal requirements for your variant. *Why:* production programming and thermal spec differences. ([Mouser Electronics][3])
5. **FTDI / programmer identification:** if you rely on Vivado Hardware Manager / FTDI programmers, make sure your toolchain supports the updated FTDI EEPROM method (post-2022 tools); Trenz updated EEPROM content for this. *Why:* programming/ID compatibility. ([Trenz Electronic][5])
6. **Keep testpoints & standard fiducials** — rev-4 standardized and added testpoints; mirror this in your carrier for easier manufacturing. *Why:* assembly and debug efficiency. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
7. **If you plan to remove HDMI/DSI/CSI or other rev-4 features, check for shared clocks / signals** — Trenz removed HDMI CEC and marked some parts DNP; ensure you don’t remove globally shared oscillators or clocks used by the PS. *Why:* shared resources can quietly break other functions. ([Mouser Electronics][2])
8. **Document and track module serial / revision numbers in BOM/test records** — PCN-targeting may be serial-range dependent (FTDI PCN lists serial > 967172). *Why:* to know which PCNs affect which boards in production. ([Trenz Electronic][5])

